Sculpt and rig the **Sunfront Bulwark Foundry**, a heavy armored bunker-forge whose
blast door raises and whose flywheel turns on their own, as a 66x56x66 opaque-voxel
model using only the `voxel-anim` tool. You build it by **painting discrete opaque
cells** — one recorded operation at a time — and you **author its two self-playing
animations** as F-curves.

This test measures **creativity and craft**, not instruction-following: the brief
tells you *what the Foundry is* and *how it must move*, but the parts you build, how
you break the foundry into moving pieces, where you place the joints, and how you
shape every form are **entirely yours to invent**. Work out the pieces a bunker-forge
with a raising door and a turning wheel needs.

## How sculpting and rigging works

The only way to place a voxel and edit the rig is the `voxel-anim` binary already on
your `PATH`. You build the model by calling it **one operation at a time**, and the
ordered operations you issue — recorded per part to `parts/<part>.actions.json` —
together with the rig you define are your submission.

Every voxel operation targets a **part** with `--part <name>`; each part is sculpted
**separately** in its own preview and log. Voxels are **opaque** `#rrggbb` colors —
there is no transparency. Each call only records the operation to that part's log and
renders **nothing** — run `voxel-anim render` to (re)draw the previews and the
assembled scene (`--component <part>` redraws one part; `--time <ms> --animation
<name>` renders the model posed at an instant of an animation), and you **must** run
`voxel-anim render` before you finish so it emits the per-part `.glb` geometry the
run is scored from — an unrendered part is scored as empty.

**You invent the rig.** Define whatever parts you need with `define-part` (giving each
a parent and a pivot), place joints on them with `define-joint`, and author the two
required animations with `define-animation`/`add-keyframe`. Every voxel operation
targets a **part** with `--part <name>`, so define a part before you sculpt into it;
each part is a **separate field** sculpted in the shared volume's coordinates. The
volume starts **empty**.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its part, for example:

```
voxel-anim define-part --name body
voxel-anim fill-box --part body --x0 6 --y0 0 --z0 6 --x1 53 --y1 44 --z1 53 --color "#c69a4b"
```

(The part name above is just an example — name your own parts however you like.) Run
`voxel-anim --help` to list every operation **and** the rig subcommands (defining
parts, setting pivots, defining joints, and authoring animations' F-curve keyframes
with `define-animation`/`add-keyframe`), and `voxel-anim <operation> --help` for one
operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative contract. **Read
`parts/<part>.png` after you run `voxel-anim render`** to see that part's progress, **and read the
assembled-scene previews under `scene/` (`scene/iso.png`, `scene/front.png`,
`scene/side.png`, `scene/top.png`)** — the whole foundry composed from all your parts,
drawn when you run `voxel-anim render`. The scene views are how you confirm the parts
actually fit together: that the blast door sits square in its front opening and the
flywheel seats on its flank. Because each part is sculpted in the shared volume's
coordinates, position each part where it belongs on the finished building; the scene
simply composes them there.

